  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Chef Barinov proudly serves his delicate auteur dishes to the district prefect, but the official mocks the tiny portions as mere starters and demands his ribs—triggering Barinov's famous temper.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну как вам блюдо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Для начала пойдет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Так что тащи уже ребрышки.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Высокая кухня — это вам не закуска.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "А в-третьих, что, Витя?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не Витя, а Виктор Петрович.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Зря ты так, Виктор Петрович.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Земля ведь круглая.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А оплатить кто будет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "А я и не ел ничего.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Я еще вернусь сюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Скатертью дорога!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну как вам блюдо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, how do you like the dish?' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'come on' and 'how' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Для начала пойдет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'For a starter it will do.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'for' and finishing on 'to go'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Так что тащи уже ребрышки.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'So bring out the ribs already.' Notice how Russian expresses 'so' and 'ribs' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Высокая кухня — это вам не закуска.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Haute cuisine is not a snack for you.' The grammar packs 'high' and 'appetizer' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "А в-третьих, что, Витя?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And thirdly, what, Vitya?' Notice how Russian expresses 'and' and 'what' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не Витя, а Виктор Петрович.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Not Vitya, but Viktor Petrovich.' Chef Barinov anchors the line on 'not', while 'and' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Зря ты так, Виктор Петрович.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You should not act like that, Viktor Petrovich.' The meaning hinges on 'for nothing' working together with 'so' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Земля ведь круглая.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'After all, the earth is round.' The meaning hinges on 'ground' working together with 'round' in spoken Russian. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А оплатить кто будет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And who is going to pay?' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'and' and 'to pay' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "А я и не ел ничего.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why, I did not even eat anything.' In Russian, the idea of 'and' flows naturally into 'nothing' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Я еще вернусь сюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will be back here yet.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'I' and finishing on 'here'. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Скатертью дорога!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good riddance!' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'like a tablecloth' before landing on 'road'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
